# Conclusion

The application was vulnerable to an **Insecure Direct Object Reference (IDOR)** affecting downloadable chat transcripts.

Due to missing authorization checks, an attacker could obtain a transcript containing credentials in plain text. Those credentials were then used to authenticate successfully as another user, demonstrating how seemingly low-risk information disclosure vulnerabilities can lead directly to account compromise.

---

## Key Takeaways

- Every downloadable resource must be protected by server-side authorization.
- Customer support systems must never expose or confirm user passwords.
- Sensitive information should never be stored in downloadable transcripts.
- Predictable resource identifiers increase the likelihood of unauthorized access.
- Broken Access Control remains one of the most critical web application vulnerabilities.

